faq:
  - q: "What is the Seattle job market like in 2026?"
    a: "Seattle is the third-largest tech market in the US, anchored by two of the world's most valuable companies: Amazon (headquartered in South Lake Union) and Microsoft (headquartered in Redmond). The Seattle-Bellevue-Redmond metro area also hosts major offices for Google (Kirkland), Meta (Bellevue), Apple (Seattle and Bellevue), Salesforce, and hundreds of tech and biotech companies. Beyond tech, Seattle's economy includes Boeing (aerospace, though manufacturing has shifted to other locations), Starbucks, REI, Nordstrom, and a growing biotech and life sciences sector anchored by the Allen Institute and Fred Hutchinson Cancer Center. Washington State has no state income tax, making total compensation effectively higher than equivalent California roles for high earners."
  - q: "How does Amazon recruiting work for software engineers and PMs?"
    a: "Amazon has one of the most distinctive interview processes in tech. Key elements: (1) Leadership Principles are not optional — Amazon's 16 Leadership Principles (Customer Obsession, Ownership, Invent and Simplify, etc.) are the framework for every behavioral interview question. Every answer must demonstrate specific Leadership Principles in action, using the STAR format. Candidates who prepare generic behavioral stories without LP-mapping consistently fail Amazon behavioral rounds. (2) Bar Raiser role — every Amazon loop includes a Bar Raiser, an interviewer whose sole function is to maintain Amazon's hiring bar across the organization. The Bar Raiser can override a hiring team's recommendation. Treat every interviewer as potentially the Bar Raiser. (3) Written document culture — senior Amazon roles (L6+) often include a writing exercise as part of the process; Amazon's culture prizes clear written thinking. (4) System design at scale — Amazon operates at enormous scale; system design questions reflect this: how do you design a recommendation engine for 300M users, or a distributed order management system processing 1M transactions per day."
  - q: "How does Microsoft recruiting work for software engineers?"
    a: "Microsoft's engineering interview process has evolved since Satya Nadella's growth mindset culture shift. Current format: (1) Recruiter screen — background and role fit, 20–30 minutes. (2) Coding assessment — online assessment or phone screen coding round. (3) Loop interviews — 4–5 rounds covering coding (2 rounds), system design (1 round), behavioral/cultural add (1 round), and for senior roles, a hiring manager deep-dive. Microsoft behavioral interviews use STAR format but without the rigid LP framework of Amazon — interviewers look for collaboration, learning orientation, and growth mindset stories specifically. (4) Collaboration and inclusion are evaluated explicitly — Microsoft's culture emphasizes collaborative problem-solving; candidates who interview with a highly competitive, individualistic style often receive negative feedback even when technically strong."
  - q: "What is the Seattle biotech and life sciences job market?"
    a: "Seattle has a growing but underappreciated biotech and life sciences sector: (1) Allen Institute for Brain Science and Allen Institute for Cell Science — world-class research organizations that hire scientists, engineers, and program managers with a research mission orientation. (2) Fred Hutchinson Cancer Center — one of the leading cancer research institutions in the US; clinical, scientific, and administrative roles. (3) Juno Therapeutics (now part of Bristol-Myers Squibb), Seagen (now Pfizer), and dozens of smaller biotech companies clustered in South Lake Union and Bothell. (4) Biotech compensation in Seattle is higher than many coastal biotech clusters outside Boston's Kendall Square and South San Francisco, partially reflecting the competition for talent with large tech companies. (5) UW Medicine and UW Health Sciences are major employers of healthcare and research professionals in the region."
  - q: "What should Washington State job seekers know about no-income-tax advantages?"
    a: "Washington State has no state income tax — a significant advantage for high earners, particularly those with large equity compensation packages. Key considerations: (1) RSU and stock option income is not taxed at the state level in Washington — for Amazon, Microsoft, and other tech employees receiving large equity packages, this creates meaningful after-tax income advantages over California or New York equivalents. (2) Washington does have a state capital gains tax (7%) on long-term capital gains above $262,000, enacted in 2023 — relevant for investors and those selling vested RSUs or options at a gain. (3) When comparing a Seattle offer to an offer in California or New York, model after-federal-plus-state tax income carefully — the effective difference for a $300K+ earner can be $25–50K annually."
